these drivers are on strike fuel prices  in Jordan have doubled in the past year  for many it's making life a daily  struggle  this Banner shows one word to sum up  their demands dignity  we joined the strike when we felt the  effects of the extremely high fuel  crisis High fuel prices mean higher  Transportation costs which in turn mean  a spike in prices of goods used by  jordanians  but while most rallies are peaceful  anger is growing  more and more people are joining the  protests often resulting in  confrontations with the police  on Thursday a senior police officer was  killed in Mount Province shot in the  head during a demonstration  the king attended the funeral a show of  support for security forces dozens of  officers have been injured in recent  unrest  the government has promised a zero  tolerance approach to violence  we are dealing with the situation and we  won't spare any effort to crack down on  whoever destabilizes public order we are  required by law to uphold our  responsibilities and duties to preserve  public security  protesters want taxes on fuel canceled  but with global Energy prices soaring  since Russia's invasion of Ukraine the  government says it's doing all it can  it spent around 700 million dollars  capping fuel prices this year  it's also promised financial aid to  vulnerable families but these protesters  say that's made very little difference  and if conditions don't improve the  unrest is likely to grow  Vincent Monaghan Al Jazeera
 

